    netWorth: "$100M (at peak)", era: "Early Masters",
    bio: "Jesse Lauriston Livermore was born in Shrewsbury, Massachusetts, to a family of farmers. His father was a hardworking but poor man who believed only manual labor led to success. At age 14, young Jesse ran away from home rather than accept the farming life. He made his way to Boston and landed a job posting stock prices on a chalkboard at Paine Webber brokerage.\n\nDay after day, Livermore watched numbers dance across the board. He began keeping notebooks, recording price movements, and discovered something extraordinary: stocks moved in recognizable patterns. He made his first trade at 15 in a \"bucket shop,\" an informal betting parlor on stock prices, turning $5 into $3.12. Within months, he had parlayed his savings into over $1,000. He was so consistently profitable that bucket shops began banning him.\n\nIn 1898, Livermore moved to New York to trade on the real exchange. He lost everything on his first day \u2014 bucket shop timing didn't work in real markets. He went bankrupt multiple times in his twenties, each failure teaching him brutal lessons about risk. By 1907, he had rebuilt and made his first massive score: shorting the market during the Panic of 1907, earning $1 million in a single day. J.P. Morgan himself sent a messenger asking him to stop shorting.\n\nHis legendary trade came in 1929. All summer, he quietly built massive short positions as the market soared. When the Great Crash came, Livermore was perfectly positioned. He reportedly earned $100 million in weeks \u2014 roughly $1.5 billion today. Yet his personal life was a tragedy: four marriages, depression, alcoholism. He made and lost fortunes nine times. In 1940, at 63, he walked into the Sherry-Netherland Hotel cloakroom and shot himself. His note read, in part: \"I am a failure.\" His legacy endures through the classic \"Reminiscences of a Stock Operator,\" the fictionalized biography that has guided every generation of traders since.",
