    netWorth: "$2.5B (inflation adj.)", era: "Early Masters",
    bio: "Bernard Mannes Baruch was born in Camden, South Carolina, to a Jewish family. His father had been a field surgeon in the Confederate Army before becoming a doctor. The family moved to New York when Bernard was eleven. He attended City College of New York but found the classroom stifling. At nineteen, with $250 saved, he walked onto the Wall Street trading floor and began his apprenticeship.\n\nBaruch's first decade was marked by both spectacular wins and devastating losses. He made $60,000 in his first big trade, then lost nearly everything speculating on margins he didn't understand. But he studied his mistakes obsessively, developing his signature approach: buying at the point of maximum pessimism, when blood ran in the streets. He learned to detach emotion from decision-making, a rare skill that would define his career.\n\nIn 1900, at age thirty, Baruch became a millionaire. His greatest trade came during the Panic of 1907. While crowds panicked and sold everything, Baruch calmly analyzed the fundamentals of American railroads and steel companies. He recognized that the panic was temporary and the underlying businesses were stronger than ever. He bought aggressively. When the market recovered, his fortune multiplied several times over.\n\nDuring World War I, President Woodrow Wilson appointed Baruch chairman of the War Industries Board, making him one of the most powerful men in America. He oversaw the entire American industrial war effort, coordinating the production of everything from rifles to battleships. After the war, he returned to markets and correctly predicted the 1929 Crash, moving his assets into bonds while others piled into stocks. He lived to age 94, advising presidents from Wilson to Kennedy, and establishing the Baruch Foundation which continues his philanthropic legacy today.",
