    bio: "Gerald Mortimer Loeb was born in San Francisco, the son of a prosperous wine merchant. He attended Stanford University but left before graduating to join his father's business. When the 1906 earthquake destroyed San Francisco, the Loeb family lost nearly everything. Young Gerald learned early that prosperity could vanish overnight.\n\nIn 1921, Loeb moved to New York and took a job as a stockbroker at E.F. Hutton. The 1920s bull market was exhilarating, but Loeb watched in horror as colleagues borrowed heavily to buy stocks on margin. He began quietly selling his positions in 1928, convinced the market was dangerously overvalued. When the Crash came, he was one of the few on Wall Street who had preserved capital. He used his cash to buy quality companies at Depression-era prices, building a fortune that would last a lifetime.\n\nLoeb's book, \"The Battle for Investment Survival,\" published in 1935, became an instant classic. In it, he argued that most investors lose because they treat the stock market like a savings account rather than a battlefield. He advocated for quick, decisive action: cutting losses immediately, letting winners run, and never falling in love with a stock. His philosophy was contrarian to the buy-and-hold wisdom of his era, and it has been validated by decades of market behavior.",
