    netWorth: undefined, era: "Early Masters",
    bio: "Nicolas Darvas was born in Hungary and trained as an economist before becoming one half of the world's highest-paid ballroom dance team with his sister Julia. They performed across Europe and America, earning thousands per show. But Darvas had a secret obsession: the stock market. Between performances, he devoured financial newspapers and spent hours studying price charts.\n\nIn 1952, Darvas received 6,000 shares of a Canadian mining stock as payment for a performance. The stock collapsed. Rather than giving up, Darvas became determined to crack the code of the market. He studied hundreds of charts, looking for patterns in the greatest winners. He noticed that stocks often traded in tight ranges \u2014 \"boxes\" \u2014 before breaking out to new highs. His theory was simple: buy when a stock breaks out of its box to the upside, sell when it falls below.\n\nOver the next 18 months, while touring the world as a dancer, Darvas turned $36,000 into over $2 million using telegrams to communicate with his broker. He documented the entire journey in his book, \"How I Made $2,000,000 in the Stock Market,\" which became an instant classic. His story proved that systematic, rules-based trading could succeed even for someone with no Wall Street connections and a full-time career in a completely different field.",
