    bio: "Benjamin Grossbaum was born in London, but his family moved to New York when he was one. His father died when Benjamin was nine, and the family fell into poverty. This early trauma shaped his entire worldview: he would spend his life searching for safety and certainty in an uncertain world. He attended Columbia University, graduating second in his class at age 20.\n\nGraham's defining moment came in the 1929 Crash. His investment partnership lost nearly 70% of its value. He was personally devastated, nearly bankrupted. But from this crucible emerged the philosophy that changed investing forever. He began teaching at Columbia, arguing that stocks had an intrinsic value separate from their market price. Patient investors could calculate this value and buy with a \"margin of safety\" \u2014 a cushion of protection against error.\n\nIn 1934, Graham published \"Security Analysis\" with David Dodd \u2014 the bible of value investing. In 1949, he wrote \"The Intelligent Investor,\" which Warren Buffett calls \"the best book about investing ever written.\" Among his students were Warren Buffett, Irving Kahn, Walter Schloss, and Bill Ruane \u2014 who collectively managed hundreds of billions using his principles. Graham's margin of safety concept remains the cornerstone of value investing today. He died in 1976, leaving behind a legacy that shaped the thinking of every serious investor who followed.",
