    bio: "Warren Edward Buffett was born in Omaha, Nebraska, during the Great Depression. His father was a stockbroker and Congressman. At age six, Warren bought six-packs of Coca-Cola and resold them for a profit. At eleven, he bought his first stock. By thirteen, he filed his first tax return, deducting his bicycle as a business expense.\n\nAt Columbia Business School, Buffett studied under Benjamin Graham, becoming his only A+ student. In 1956, at age 25, he started his partnership with $105,000 from family and friends. He applied Graham's value principles but added a crucial insight from Philip Fisher: buy not just cheap companies, but great ones. The partnership returned 29.5% compounded annually until 1969.\n\nBuffett took control of Berkshire Hathaway, a failing textile mill, and transformed it into the world's largest conglomerate \u2014 owning insurers, railroads, and stakes in Coca-Cola, Apple, and American Express. He has compounded at roughly 20% annually for over six decades, creating one of the greatest fortunes in human history. Yet he still lives in the same house he bought in 1958 for $31,500, drinks five Cokes a day, and eats at McDonald's. He has pledged to give away 99% of his wealth, primarily through the Gates Foundation. At 94, he remains the most respected voice in investing.",
