    netWorth: "$450M", era: "Value & Growth",
    bio: "Peter Lynch grew up in Newton, Massachusetts. His father died of cancer when Peter was ten, and the family plunged from middle-class comfort to struggling overnight. His mother became a secretary; Peter caddied at golf courses, where he first heard stock tips from wealthy businessmen. He bought Flying Tiger Airlines stock with his caddie savings \u2014 it went up tenfold, paying for college at Boston College and Wharton.\n\nLynch joined Fidelity in 1966 as a research analyst, tireless in visiting companies and talking to managers. In 1977, he became manager of the Fidelity Magellan Fund, then a small $18 million fund. Under his management, Magellan became the world's largest mutual fund at $14 billion. From 1977 to 1990, Magellan returned 29.2% annually \u2014 nearly double the S&P 500. A $10,000 investment became $280,000.\n\nLynch's famous advice \u2014 \"Invest in what you know\" \u2014 democratized stock picking. If you love a product, investigate whether the company behind it is worth owning. He retired at 46 at his peak, wanting to spend time with his family. His books \"One Up on Wall Street\" and \"Beating the Street\" sold millions and introduced a generation to the stock market. He remains one of the most beloved figures in investing.",
